{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by jesse eisenberg, aaron sorkin, and stan chervin. the film is based on the true story of the founding of the social networking site facebook and the subsequent lawsuits between the site's creators and mark zuckerberg.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, a brilliant and awkward college student who creates a website called \"facemash\" that becomes a huge success on harvard university's campus. however, his site is shut down by the university's administration, and zuckerberg is left to ponder his next move.\n\nenter eduardo saverin (andrew garfield), a russian immigrant who introduces zuckerberg to the idea of creating a social networking site that would allow harvard students to create profiles and connect with one another. zuckerberg quickly becomes obsessed with the idea, and with the help of his friends, including andrew mccollum (josh zuckerman), dustin moskovitz (joseph mazzello), and christopher hughes (james franco), they begin to build a site that would eventually become facebook.\n\nthe film's tone is largely dark and cynical, with a focus on the cutthroat world of silicon valley and the ruthless business tactics employed by zuckerberg and his investors. however, the film also explores the complexities of zuckerberg's personality, revealing a brilliant and ambitious young man who is also insecure and vulnerable.\n\none of the film's greatest strengths is its performances, particularly jesse eisenberg's portrayal of mark zuckerberg. eisenberg's performance is both captivating and heartbreaking, capturing the nuances of zuckerberg's personality and his struggles with fame and success.\n\nthe film's direction is also noteworthy, with david fincher bringing his signature dark and moody style to the film. the cinematography is stunning, with a focus on capturing the beauty and isolation of harvard university's campus.\n\nthe film's screenplay, written by aaron sorkin, is also a major highlight. sorkin's dialogue is witty and engaging, capturing the banter and humor of the harvard social scene. the film's script is also surprisingly nuanced, exploring the complexities of zuckerberg's personality and the motivations behind his actions.\n\none of the film's greatest criticisms is its portrayal of the women in the film, particularly cameron and tyler (brianne howard and brooklyn decker). the film's depiction of these characters is largely one-dimensional and stereotypical, with little development or depth.\n\noverall, the social network is a compelling and thought-provoking film that explores the complexities of the tech industry and the true story behind the founding of facebook. with its stunning performances, direction, and screenplay, the film is a must-see for anyone interested in the world of technology and social media.\n\nrating: 4.5/5", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.045232724}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0016868184}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -7.6293654e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.18040486}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00012385078}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.32515162}, {"token": "201", "logprob": -2.9086643e-05}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -3.933899e-06}, {"token": " american", "logprob": -0.3342006}, {"token": " bi", "logprob": -0.0031711787}, {"token": "ographical", "logprob": -0.00043990472}, {"token": " drama", "logprob": -0.011117537}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.00057299406}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -0.18416922}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -0.0021900972}, {"token": " david", "logprob": -0.009290322}, {"token": " fin", "logprob": -0.00041380903}, {"token": "cher", "logprob": -2.682173e-05}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.2561861}, {"token": " written", "logprob": -0.0026351034}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -3.3378547e-06}, 